You previously wrote a pytest file against this contract:

```python
OPENAI_ENCODINGS: dict[str, str] = {
    "gpt-5": "o200k_base",
    "gpt-4o": "o200k_base",
    "gpt-4": "cl100k_base",
}


def count_openai(text: str, encoding: str) -> int:
    """Count `text`'s OpenAI tokens for `encoding`."""
```

One of your tests, `test_count_openai_additivity_zero_drift`, asserted that
splitting text into parts and summing `count_openai` per part equals
`count_openai` on the joined text. That assertion is WRONG and has been
measured failing: `tiktoken`'s BPE merges tokens across a split boundary
(a trailing space token from one part can fuse with the next part's first
word once the text is joined), so per-part counts do not sum to the whole
count in general. This is not a bug in `count_openai` — it is a real
property of BPE tokenization, confirmed by direct measurement:

```python
import tiktoken
enc = tiktoken.get_encoding("o200k_base")
parts = [
    "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. ",
    "Pack my box with five dozen liquor jugs. ",
    "How vexingly quick daft zebras jump! ",
    "Sphinx of black quartz, judge my vow.",
]
joined = "".join(parts)
sum(len(enc.encode(p)) for p in parts)  # == 43
len(enc.encode(joined))                  # == 40
```

Replace `test_count_openai_additivity_zero_drift` with a corrected test,
`test_count_openai_additivity_is_not_guaranteed`, that:

- Uses the exact `parts` list and `joined` string above.
- Asserts `sum(count_openai(p, "o200k_base") for p in parts) == 43` (the
  measured, golden per-part sum).
- Asserts `count_openai(joined, "o200k_base") == 40` (the measured, golden
  whole-text count).
- Asserts these two values are NOT equal to each other (documenting, not
  hiding, the drift — this is the point of the test).

Return exactly one fenced Python code block, nothing else — no prose before
or after it, no import line (the test will replace the old one inside an
existing file that already imports `tiktoken`, `OPENAI_ENCODINGS`, and
`count_openai`).
